    "fullContent": "The nuclear fuel cycle encompasses all the stages involved in the production of electricity from nuclear fission, beginning with the mining of uranium and ending with the disposal of spent nuclear fuel. Key terms in this domain include enrichment, which increases the concentration of Uranium-235 for reactor use, and reprocessing, a chemical process that recovers usable fissionable material from spent fuel. Reactor operations involve complex mechanisms like moderation and control rods, which regulate the speed and intensity of nuclear chain reactions. Heavy water and graphite are frequently used as moderators to slow down neutrons, enabling sustained fission. Understanding these technical terms is essential for students and professionals tracking advancements in clean energy, nuclear safety standards, and global non-proliferation frameworks. Mastering this vocabulary strengthens foundational knowledge required for civil services and specialized technical tests.",
