    "id": "gktoday-important-dna-markers-used-in-species-identification",
    "title": "Important DNA Markers Used in Species Identification",
    "date": "Mon, 10 Aug 2026 14:21:47 GMT",
    "category": "Science & Space",
    "summary": "DNA barcoding and molecular markers have become indispensable tools in modern wildlife conservation, forensics, and taxonomy. These genetic tools allow scientists to accurately identify species, trace illegal wildlife trade, and monitor biodiversity.",
    "keyPoints": [
      "COI (Cytochrome c oxidase subunit 1) is the standard DNA barcode region used for identifying animal species.",
      "ITS (Internal Transcribed Spacer) regions are widely utilized for molecular identification in fungi and plants.",
      "Microsatellites and SNPs help researchers study population genetics, kinship, and genetic diversity."
    ],
    "keyHighlights": [
      "COI (Cytochrome c oxidase subunit 1) is the standard DNA barcode region used for identifying animal species.",
      "ITS (Internal Transcribed Spacer) regions are widely utilized for molecular identification in fungi and plants.",
      "Microsatellites and SNPs help researchers study population genetics, kinship, and genetic diversity."
    ],
    "fullContent": "The application of molecular biology in conservation science has revolutionized how researchers identify and classify living organisms. DNA markers provide an objective, foolproof method for species identification, particularly when dealing with fragmented samples, processed animal parts, or cryptic species that look morphologically identical. In the animal kingdom, the mitochondrial gene known as COI (Cytochrome c oxidase subunit 1) serves as the universal DNA barcode. A short sequence of this gene can match unknown tissue samples against comprehensive global reference libraries, aiding customs authorities in curbing illegal wildlife trafficking. For plants and fungi, nuclear ribosomal ITS regions are preferred due to their appropriate rates of evolutionary divergence. Furthermore, advanced markers like Single Nucleotide Polymorphisms (SNPs) and microsatellites enable conservationists to assess population viability, track endangered wildlife lineages, and implement targeted breeding or reintroduction programs. These genetic insights are vital for formulating effective biodiversity conservation strategies in a rapidly changing environment.",
    "source": "GK Today",
    "sourceUrl": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiggFBVV95cUxNMk14SlQtQ0dGZEJRb1lGX2lKNzhxcWJsR2xWc3pMbUdja1lGLUJidU9GaE1aRGEwZzhWeW9rcXc0YjdGa3FYYUhZSHhURXVtbUNib21RVmpJcWR6WW5ZWFh3VTl4UHcwU1JMMVVSTmJ4VXF0emhtMjZ6bGJURUdLU2Rn?oc=5",
    "publishedAt": "Mon, 10 Aug 2026 14:21:47 GMT",
    "syncedAt": "2026-08-14T08:11:38.781Z"
