    "fullContent": "### Background and Cabinet Approval In a landmark decision to bolster India's clean energy transition, the Union Cabinet, chaired by the Prime Minister, approved the Phase-II expansion of the **National Green Hydrogen Mission (NGHM)** on 11 August 2026. The Phase-II framework introduces an additional financial allocation of **₹8,500 crore**, raising the total commitment of the central government to over ₹28,000 crore. ### Core Objectives and Strategic Enhancements The expanded mission shifts focus toward commercial scale-up, export infrastructure, and deeper integration into hard-to-abate industrial sectors like steel, shipping, and fertilizer manufacturing: - **Green Ammonia Export Hubs:** Three major maritime facilities—Paradip Port (Odisha), Deendayal Port (Kandla, Gujarat), and VO Chidambaranar Port (Tuticorin, Tamil Nadu)—will be developed as world-class export terminals. - **SIGHT Program Expansion:** The Strategic Interventions for Green Hydrogen Transition (SIGHT) scheme receives enhanced funding to scale domestic electrolyzer manufacturing capacity to 15 GW per annum by 2030. - **Revised Targets:** India now aims to generate **10 Million Metric Tonnes (MMT)** of Green Hydrogen annually by 2030, supported by approximately 220 GW of dedicated renewable energy capacity. ### Exam Relevance & Key Takeaways - **Implementing Ministry:** Ministry of New and Renewable Energy (MNRE). - **Target Year:** 2030 for primary target fulfillment (reducing cumulative fossil fuel imports by over ₹1 lakh crore). - **UPSC/PSC Focus:** Useful for GS-III (Environment, Energy Infrastructure) and objective questions on scheme components (SIGHT, hub locations, and capacity targets).",
